   "id": "e2-84",
   "name": "Nouns as modifiers"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "A noun can be used before another noun to describe it, like an adjective. It is then a noun modifier.\n• a school bag (a bag for school), a football match, a dance festival, a leather belt, a village chief, a health centre.\nThe main noun is the last one; the first noun tells what kind: a bus station is a station (for buses).\nThe modifier is usually singular even when the meaning is plural: a shoe shop (it sells many shoes), a ten-kilometre race, a two-day festival.\nCompare: a coffee cup (a cup for coffee) and a cup of coffee (a cup full of coffee).",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Write a noun phrase with a noun modifier for “a race of ten kilometres”.",
  "steps": [
   "The main noun is “race”; the modifier goes before it.",
   "Numbers + noun modifiers stay singular and take a hyphen: a ten-kilometre race."
  ],
  "answer": "a ten-kilometre race",
  "checks": []
 },
